#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "345d6df7-2ada-5c2e-8146-7c779afae3af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e930a29a-6adf-5aaa-9b44-86dd8d570ae7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5354" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$4 y^2  - 10 x^2 - 8 y  - 40 x  = 76?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}-8y-40x & =76 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-10{x}^{2}-40x & =76 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-10[{x}^{2}+4x] & =76 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-10[(x+2)^{2}-4] & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-10(x+2)^{2}+40 & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =76-40+4 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f99d4fec-bc58-5a09-9fac-38b46f233489" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "0caa1041-a564-5428-9838-20cea32fdf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-6775" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} - \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1 ?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdab0134-e416-51cf-89fa-73ad4a2c2eb9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "8e31f5b5-ff87-5c45-b143-66dfdcb4ab8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6776" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(y+3)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9} = 1 ?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-(-3))^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{9}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h = 1$ and $k=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "223e29c9-1bb5-51ee-9ec8-4d5c15363951" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given an Equation in Expanded Form"}] :question/id #uuid "af591507-e248-53d2-a391-e053cdc3fdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-6777" :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/57e159560b79e8383e6a2eec7a8f8dd6568fbf0fb8dae9f5277d35a166b6402a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/6c305528045f15b88bbb08bcbb70e321036d4c901b971d2d9f37ec0fc14c5bab.png) is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/42df0f3278c4cf754f5e3c65787c2640a0c084ab1e79874dd3c071a97012b0dd.png)\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037937/assets/6c64f050494f28920cf5518293243a78d46e5fa2758fc8f470fdb466586effbc.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/140379